---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

import Header from "../../components/shared/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/shared/Footer.astro";
import PostHeader from "../../components/blog/PostHeader.astro";

import "../../styles/blog/blog-post.css"
---

<Layout
>

    <Header 
        links={[
            {label: "Home", href: "/"},
            {label: "All Articles", href: "/blog"}
        ]}
    />

    <main id="top">
        <article class="blog-post">
            <div class="container">
                
                <PostHeader 
                    title="Notetaking as a habit: How it has helped me",
                    date="28-08-2026",
                    tags={["Life", "Learning", "Habits"]}
                />

                <div class="post-content">

                    <h2>
                        Context
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        During the last academic year I stopped note-taking, which was something I had tried hard to sustain but ultimately couldn't.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        This August I decided to get back into the habit.
                        Not because it made me feel productive, but because I had started to realise how useful it was to have a record of what I was actually doing.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        When I was taking notes consistently, I found that they gave me something I couldn't really get from memory alone, 
                        a way to look back at my own behaviour and identify patterns.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        In particular, it helped me:
                    </p>

                    <ul class="indented">
                        <li>Improve my productivity</li>
                        <li>Understand why some days went badly</li>
                        <li>Manage my time more realistically</li>
                        <li>Notice patterns that I otherwise would have ignored</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h2>
                        How I take notes
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        I disagree with the notion that people should spend a long time finding and perfecting a note-taking method.
                        In my opinion, any system that keeps you writing consistently is a good one.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        For me, that system is simply a daily log in Obsidian.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        What matters much more than the software or structure is the value of what you write down.
                        Writing a monotonous narration of everything I did that day would be fairly useless.
                        I want my notes to help me understand the day, not just record it.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I generally split my logs into two sections: <strong>tasks</strong> and <strong>how the day went</strong>.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The tasks section is simple.
                        I write down what I want to get done that day and use it as a reference throughout the day.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The second section is where most of the value is. 
                        Rather than only recording <em>if</em> I completed something, I try to record <em>how</em> I completed it.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        For example, if I spend two hours on something that should have taken thirty minutes, that is much more useful information than simply writing "completed task".
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        It gives me something to investigate.
                        Did I underestimate the task?
                        Did I get distracted?
                        Was I tired?
                        Did I spend too long trying to solve something in the wrong way?
                    </p>

                    <h2>
                        It made my productivity less subjective
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        One of the biggest benefits has been being able to look at my productivity more objectively.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Without notes, I might finish a day feeling like I had been unproductive without really knowing why.
                        Alternatively, I might feel like I had worked hard simply because I had spent most of the day at my desk.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I can look back and see what I actually intended to do, what I actually did, and how long certain things took.
                        That makes it much easier to identify where a day went wrong.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        This has also changed how I plan future days.
                        If I repeatedly give myself five tasks and only realistically have enough time for three,
                        the problem probably isn't that I need to become dramatically more productive. 
                        I may simply be planning badly.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The notes give me evidence for that instead of forcing me to rely on how productive I <em>feel</em>.
                    </p>

                    <h2>
                        It helped me notice patterns
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        This is probably the part of note-taking that I value the most.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        A single bad day isn't particularly informative.
                        You might be tired, distracted, busy, or simply have an unusually difficult day.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        However, when the same thing appears repeatedly in your notes, it becomes difficult to dismiss.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I have found this particularly useful for things like sleep and productivity.
                        Instead of vaguely remembering that I "haven't been sleeping well lately", I can look back at what actually happened and see whether there is a pattern.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The same applies to studying.
                        If I repeatedly write that I struggled to concentrate at a particular point in the day,
                        or that a certain type of task consistently takes longer than expected, that is useful information for planning around.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        In that sense, my notes have gradually become less like a diary and more like a dataset about myself.
                    </p>

                    <h2>
                        It made reflection easier
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        Another unexpected benefit is that reflection requires much less effort when the information is already written down.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Instead of asking myself "How has this week gone?", I can actually look at the week and see it.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        That changes the quality of the question.
                        I am no longer trying to reconstruct several days from memory.
                        I can compare what I planned to do with what actually happened and think about why there was a difference.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        This has made mistakes considerably more useful.
                        A bad day doesn't just become a bad day that I forget about. I can understand what caused it and potentially avoid repeating it.
                    </p>

                    <h2>
                        The real benefit isn't the notes
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        Ultimately, I don't think the important part of this habit is note-taking itself.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The most useful part is how much better my mindset has become. 
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I focus on learning something everyday, rather than just completing tasks for the sake of it. 
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        For example, the old me would just read an engineering blog, but then forget about it a week later. Now I'm proactively reading engineering blogs
                        (such as my personal favourite, <a href="https://planetscale.com/blog/category/engineering">PlanetScale's blogs</a>) and writing down key engineering
                        principles. 
                    </p>

                    <p> 
                        The only reason I learn from this is because I go through the past week with every new week, exploring what I did, what I learnt and how it could apply to
                        anything I've experienced, such as building Crestr.  
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        This is probably the main reason I've come back to note-taking.
                    </p>

                </div>

                <footer class="post-footer">
                    <div class="post-footer-nav">
                        <a href="/blog" class="post-footer-link">← All Articles</a>
                    </div>
                </footer>

            </div>

        </article>

    </main>

    <Footer />
</Layout>